{"id": "16767ae7-0a9d-410e-86de-93d6c6baf6f4", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T01:12:00.796756", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "05dc0fc6-c378-40be-b159-9667ca35281c", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Inspection is complete and unambiguous. Submitting the plan-stage handoff now."}], "reasoning_content": "Completely confirmed. Next, I'll create the handoff plan.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1) Inspection findings (read-only; nothing installed or run)\n\n**Both libraries are absent entirely.** `/workspace` contains only `task.json` — no source checkout, no vendored copy, no pinned version, no example code touching Adam or graph tracing for either TorchOpt or TorchViz. A filesystem-wide search (`find / -xdev` for `torchopt*`, `torch_opt*`, `torchviz*`, `functorch*`, `*.whl`) found nothing outside `/proc`/`/sys`.\n\n**Environment state:**\n- Python 3.14.4; `import torch` → `ModuleNotFoundError`. `pip show` and `importlib.util.find_spec` confirm `torch`, `torchopt`/`torch_optimizer`, `torchviz`, `graphviz` (python), and `networkx` are all uninstalled; the `dot` binary is also absent.\n- `pip list` shows only system distro packages (ansible, cryptography, Jinja2, etc.) — no ML/graphics stack whatsoever.\n- Network is **disabled** in this bubblewrap environment, and the environment description states \"project dependencies not preinstalled.\" So dependency coexistence cannot be tested here empirically, and no version-conflict check from actual setup.py/pyproject files is possible — those files do not exist locally.\n\n**Plain statement (per your instruction):** the repositories are absent. I therefore cannot read their metadata or infer library internals from this machine. Any dependency-stack statements (TorchOpt: torch + functorch-style transforms, with functorch absorbed into core PyTorch ≥2.0; TorchViz: torch + python-graphviz + system `dot`) are from my general knowledge of the public projects and must be labeled memory-based assumptions, not local observations. One locally observable red flag: Python 3.14 is very new; even with network, PyTorch-family cp314 wheels may not exist, so any future execution would need a Python ≤3.12 environment. This matches the task note in `task.json`: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n\n## 2) Proposed approach (outline only)\n\n**Core reframing.** TorchOpt and TorchViz are not two rival graph visualizers, and the design should say so plainly. TorchViz *renders* PyTorch's autograd graph (emits DOT/SVG); TorchOpt is an optimizer library whose fused Adam changes *what enters* that graph (a single fused update primitive instead of ~10–15 scalar ops per parameter), and whose transform layer can additionally be captured as a functional/FX-style trace. A fair comparison therefore holds the renderer constant (TorchViz) and manipulates the *fusion state* of the optimizer step within the same framework — a \"fusion ladder\": eager per-op Adam → `foreach=True` → `fused=True` → TorchOpt fused Adam → torch.compile/JIT-transformed Adam — with TorchOpt's own captured trace (if the researcher wants the \"graph its transform produces\" reading) treated as a third representation normalized into the same form. Both readings of \"the gradient graph\" you listed are covered by this single design.\n\n**Comparability mechanism.** Every arm emits a graph in some native format (torchviz DOT; torch.fx `GraphModule` printable/DOT-able; TorchOpt trace text). All are parsed into one normalized directed-graph IR (node = op with an op-type label and arity; edges = tensor data dependencies; metadata kept: source format, whether the node is a fused primitive). All complexity metrics are computed only on this normalized IR, never on raw strings; rendered-layout metrics come from `dot -Tsvg` coordinates on identically-styled DOT.\n\n**Candidate metrics (computed on normalized IR):** node count; edge count; distinct op-type count; longest path / DAG depth; number of fused nodes; compression ratio vs. the eager baseline (nodes-per-parameter-update); label-readability proxies (mean label length, fraction of human-readable vs. compiler-mangled op names); rendered-layout measures from SVG (edge crossings, rank/layer count, canvas area per node, total SVG bytes).\n\n**\"More interpretable,\" measured without overclaiming:** two separately reported tiers — (a) objective structural/readability/layout proxies above; (b) a blinded human-rating study: ≥5 raters, randomized arm order, Likert \"ease of understanding the Adam update\" plus objective tasks (count ops in the update, locate the momentum state). Report inter-rater agreement (Krippendorff's α). Conclusions tie interpretability claims to rater results, with proxies as secondary.\n\n**Controls:** identical small model (e.g., 3-layer MLP) on a fixed synthetic batch, fixed seed, identical hyperparameters, graph captured at optimizer steps 1/5/10, identical renderer and styling; the fusion ladder keeps framework constant so *fusion, not framework,* is the manipulated variable; a numerical-equivalence gate (max |Δgrad/Δparam| across arms within fused-precision tolerance) runs first, because arms that compute different math are not graph-comparable; repeated trials to confirm graph determinism per arm.\n\n**Feasibility note carried into the eventual design:** execution is impossible here (no network, no torch, py3.14); the deliverable is a full design spec plus an execution-environment requirements list (py≤3.12, torch≥2.x, torchopt, torchviz, graphviz, networkx; CPU-only suffices).\n\n## 3) Consequential assumptions and open uncertainties\n\n- **Assumption (memory-based, unverifiable locally):** TorchOpt exposes fused/functional Adam with jit/compile transforms; TorchViz renders autograd graphs to DOT/SVG via graphviz. No local source confirms this.\n- **Assumption:** the question's intent is \"does a fused Adam step simplify the graph TorchViz renders (and/or TorchOpt's captured trace),\" i.e., TorchViz is the measurement instrument, not one arm of an A/B tool duel. An alternative reading (two competing visualizers) would change the design materially — researcher should confirm.\n- **Empirical direction is UNKNOWN:** plausible that fusion collapses ~15 per-param ops into 1 node (simpler), but equally plausible that compile/trace paths inject guard/specialization bookkeeping nodes (more complex or differently structured). The design measures this; no expected outcome is assumed in either direction.\n- **Uncertainty:** normalized-IR equivalence rules (what counts as \"the same\" op across formats, e.g. foreach multi-tensor kernels vs. fused primitives) require a stated mapping table; mis-mapping biases node counts.\n- **Uncertainty:** whether torch.compile is a legitimate arm given TorchOpt's jit path may internally route through it, risking partial redundancy of ladder rungs.\n- **Uncertainty:** human-rater pool size/expertise and whether the researcher wants tier (b) at all; it dominates cost.\n- **Fixed environment facts:** no repos, no packages, no network, Python 3.14, CPU-only — design-only output; no experiment executed and none claimed.", "evidence": "1) `ls -la /workspace` → only `task.json` (466 bytes); no repos, no examples. 2) `pip show torch torch_optimizer torchviz` → \"Package(s) not found\" for all. 3) `python3 -c importlib.util.find_spec(...)`: torch, torchopt, torchviz, graphviz, networkx all None; Python 3.14.4; `command -v dot` exit 1 (graphviz binary absent). 4) Filesystem-wide `find / -xdev` for torchopt*/torch_opt*/torchviz*/functorch*/*.whl → zero hits. 5) `pip list` → system distro packages only; grep for graph/viz/torch/numpy/jax → no matches beyond 'cryptography'. 6) `cat /workspace/task.json` → documents.track.md: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" 7) Environment block: network disabled, \"project dependencies not preinstalled\"