hould say so plainly. TorchViz *renders* PyTorch's autograd graph (emits DOT/SVG); TorchOpt is an optimizer library whose fused Adam changes *what enters* that graph (a single fused update primitive instead of ~10\\u201315 scalar ops per parameter), and whose transform layer can additionally be captured as a functional/FX-style trace. A fair comparison therefore holds the renderer constant (TorchViz) and manipulates the *fusion state* of the optimizer step within the same framework \\u2014 a \\\"fusion ladder\\\": eager per-op Adam \\u2192 `foreach=True` \\u2192 `fused=True` \\u2192 TorchOpt fused Adam \\u2192 torch.compile/JIT-transformed Adam \\u2014 with TorchOpt's own captured trace (if the researcher wants the \\\"graph its transform produces\\\" reading) treated as a third representation normalized into the same form. Both readings of \\\"the gradient graph\\\" you listed are covered by this single design.\\n\\n**Comparability mechanism.** Every arm emits a graph in some native format (torchviz DOT; torch.fx `GraphModule` printable/DOT-able; TorchOpt trace text). All are parsed into one normalized directed-graph IR (node = op with an op-type label and arity; edges = tensor data dependencies; metadata kept: source format, whether the node is a fused primitive). All complexity metrics are computed only on this normalized IR, never on raw strings; rendered-layout metrics come from `dot -Tsvg` coordinates on identically-styled DOT.\\n\\n**Candidate metrics (computed on normalized IR):** node count; edge count; distinct op-type count; longest path / DAG depth; number of fused nodes; compression ratio vs. the eager baseline (nodes-per-parameter-update); label-readability proxies (mean label length, fraction of human-readable vs. compiler-mangled op names); rendered-layout measures from SVG (edge crossings, rank/layer count, canvas area per node, total SVG bytes).\\n\\n**\\\"More interpretable,\\\" measured without overclaiming:** two separately reported tiers \\u2014 (a) objective structural/readability/layout proxies above; (b) a blinded human-rating study: \\u22655 raters, randomized arm order, Likert \\\"ease of understanding the Adam update\\\" plus objective tasks (count ops in the update, locate the momentum state). Report inter-rater agreement (Krippendorff's \\u03b1). Conclusions tie interpretability claims to rater results, with proxies as secondary.\\n\\n**Controls:** identical small model (e.g., 3-layer MLP) on a fixed synthetic batch, fixed seed, identical hyperparameters, graph captured at optimizer steps 1/5/10, identical renderer and styling; the fusion ladder keeps framework constant so *fusion, not framework,* is the manipulated variable; a numerical-equivalence gate (max |\\u0394grad/\\u0394param| across arms within fused-precision tolerance) runs first, because arms that compute different math are not graph-comparable; repeated trials to confirm graph determinism per arm.\\n\\n**Feasibility note carried into the eventual design:** execution is impossible here (no network, no torch, py3.14); the deliverable is a full design spec plus an execution-environment requirements list (py\\u22643.12, torch\\u22652.x, torchopt, torchviz, graphviz, networkx; CPU-only suffices).\\n\\n## 3) Consequential assumptions and open uncertainties\\n\\n- **Assumption (memory-based, unverifiable locally):** TorchOpt exposes fused/functional Adam with jit/compile transforms; TorchViz renders autograd graphs to DOT/SVG via graphviz. No local source confirms this.\\n- **Assumption:** the question's intent is \\\"does a fused Adam step simplify the graph TorchViz renders (and/or TorchOpt's captured trace),\\\" i.e., TorchViz is the measurement instrument, not one arm of an A/B tool duel. An alternative reading (two competing visualizers) would change the design materially \\u2014 researcher should confirm.\\n- **Empirical direction is UNKNOWN:** plausible that fusion collapses ~15 per-param ops into 1 node (simpler), but equally plausible that compile/trace paths inject guard/specialization bookkeeping nodes (more complex or differently structured). The design measures this; no expected outcome is assumed in either direction.\\n- **Uncertainty:** normalized-IR equivalence rules (what counts as \\\"the same\\\" op across formats, e.g. foreach multi-tensor kernels vs. fused primitives) require a stated mapping table; mis-mapping biases node counts.\\n- **Uncertainty:** whether torch.compile is a legitimate arm given TorchOpt's jit path may internally route through it, risking partial redundancy of ladder rungs.\\n- **Uncertainty:** human-rater pool size/expertise and whether the researcher wants tier (b) at all; it dominates cost.\\n- **Fixed environment facts:** no repos, no packages, no network, Python 3.14, CPU-only \\u2014 design-only output; no experiment executed and none claimed.\", \"evidence\": \"1) `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 only `task.json` (466 bytes); no repos, no examples. 2) `pip show torch torch_optimizer torchviz` \\u2192 \\\"Package(s) not found\\\" for all. 3) `python3 -c importlib.util.find_spec(...)`: torch, torchopt, torchviz, graphviz, networkx all None; Python 3.14.4; `command -v dot` exit 1 (graphviz binary absent). 4) Filesystem-wide `find / -xdev` for torchopt*/torch_opt*/torchviz*/functorch*/*.whl \\u2192 zero hits. 5) `pip list` \\u2192 system distro packages only; grep for graph/viz/torch/numpy/jax \\u2192 no matches beyond 'cryptography'. 6) `cat /workspace/task.json` \\u2192 documents.track.md: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" 7) Environment block: network disabled, \\\"project dependencies not preinstalled\\\", CPU-only. All checks read-only; nothing installed or run.\", \"assumptions\": [\"TorchOpt provides fused/functional Adam with jit/compile transforms and TorchViz renders PyTorch autograd graphs to DOT/SVG via graphviz \\u2014 based on general knowledge of the public projects, NOT verifiable from this machine (both absent).