{"id": "4b79547d-d160-4218-9db9-e25f2a5f73fb", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T01:12:15.527711", "source": "user", "parent_id": "1dcc3b84-ae4e-4897-be5c-3832d7c424ce", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach accepted — the fusion-ladder reframing is the right call, and I confirm your interpretation of the question: TorchViz is the measurement instrument for the rendered gradient graph, and optimizer fusion state (including TorchOpt's fused Adam and its captured transform trace) is the manipulated variable. We are not staging a two-visualizer duel. Please now write the full design. Carry these specific requirements into it, without padding the scope beyond what the question needs:\\n\\n1) Fusion ladder, tightened. Keep the ladder, but state explicitly for each rung what mechanism is being varied (per-op eager ops, multi-tensor/foreach batching, a fused CUDA/one-kernel update, TorchOpt's functional/fused Adam, TorchOpt's transform/jit-captured trace) and note where a rung may be redundant with another because TorchOpt's jit path may route through torch.compile. If a rung is only a partial manipulation of \\\"fusion,\\\" label it as such rather than treating it as an independent arm. Say plainly that TorchOpt's captured trace and TorchViz's rendered autograd graph are different representations and report them as separate measurements, not one averaged \\\"graph complexity\\\".\\n\\n2) Normalization contract is part of the deliverable, not an implementation detail. Include the op-identity mapping rules as a written table (which native op labels collapse to the same IR node type; how a multi-tensor or fused primitive maps; whether constant/attribute/guard nodes are counted, hoisted, or dropped) and a stated rule for how per-parameter vs per-graph node counts are reported, so node-count differences from arity differences cannot masquerade as fusion benefits. Call out the residual bias these rules introduce.\\n\\n3) Interpretability. Include the human-rating tier but scope it as an optional, separately costed module with a stated default and a fallback if raters are unavailable (e.g. only report structural/layout/readability proxies and label interpretability claims as untested). Keep the primary conclusion anchored to what the design can actually support, and do not let proxies silently stand in for measured interpretability.\\n\\n4) Numerical-equivalence gate: specify the tolerance basis and what happens to an arm that fails it (excluded from graph comparison, reported separately).\\n\\n5) Feasibility. Keep the execution-environment requirements list, and be explicit that nothing was executed here and that the graph-complexity numbers are unspecified predictions, not results. The empirical direction stays an open question in the writeup.\\n\\nDeliver the final experiment design with rationale, controls, analysis plan (including how you'd handle non-determinism across repeated captures and the pre-registered comparison unit), and feasibility. No fabricated results, no claimed runs.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "4877c541-cb37-4b53-add1-e6fb5e20eec8", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T01:15:02.828688", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "4b79547d-d160-4218-9db9-e25f2a5f73fb", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "All inspection facts are already established from my prior read-only checks; no further exploration is needed before writing the design. Composing the full design now."}], "reasoning_content": "(Thinking) The researcher has approved and is asking for the complete design in a result handoff. All investigation is complete, and no further checks are needed, but a brief re-confirmation is unnecessary. I should write a comprehensive, well-organized experiment design covering the following:\n\n1. A narrowing of the fusion ladder and explicit mechanisms per rung. Redundancy caveats (TorchOpt jit → torch.compile), labeling of partial manipulations.\n2. Normalization contract: a written op-identity mapping table, counting rules for per-parameter vs. per-graph, residual bias.\n3. Interpretability: optional human-evaluation tier with defaults and fallbacks; primary conclusions tied to structural metrics.\n4. Numerical equivalence gate: tolerance criteria, handling of failures.\n5. Feasibility: environment requirements, nothing executed, empirical direction is undetermined.\n6. Analysis plan: pre-registered comparison units, handling of non-determinism across repeated captures.\n\nLet me design carefully.\n\n### Experimental Unit and Stimuli\n- Model: a small MLP. 3-layer fully-connected MLP (e.g., 64→32→32→1), with ReLU and MSE loss on fixed synthetic data (deterministically generated with seeded torch.randn). CPU-capable. But note: fused Adam kernels are typically CUDA-only — torch.optim.Adam(fused=True) requires CUDA; TorchOpt's fused Adam likewise. The environment is stated as cpu_affinity 224, but the target execution environment for this design is a different machine with GPU. Need to state: the execution environment needs at least one CUDA GPU; CPU fallback for fused rungs may be impossible → label as missing rung. Indeed torch's fused=True asserts CUDA tensors. TorchOpt has both (functional implementation) — torchopt's adam has a fused option that uses torch's fused kernels or a JIT path. So the design should require a CUDA-capable GPU (any recent single consumer GPU suffices). Keep the model tiny so a GPU is overkill but still fine.\n\n- Captures at optimizer steps 1, 5, 10 (stateless functional optimizers may require explicit state). TorchOpt is functional: state is external.\n\n### Ladder Rungs\nR0. Reference (no optimizer in graph): backward graph of loss only — render with torchviz before any optimizer step; measures the \"gradient graph\" without update ops. This is the floor/baseline for how much optimizer ops add.\nR1. Per-op eager Adam: torch.optim.Adam(..., foreach=False, fused=False), default math via individual aten ops (mul, add, sqrt, div...). Mechanism: baseline, per-element eager ops.\nR2. foreach Adam: foreach=True. Mechanism: multi-tensor kernel batching — partial fusion: batches across parameters but doesn't fuse across ops within an update. Label as \"partial manipulation\" (axis of batching, not op fusion).\nR3. Native fused Adam: torch.optim.Adam(fused=True). Mechanism: single fused CUDA kernel per update group; requires CUDA. Partial: opaque kernel — the autograd graph shows one fused node (or, since optimizers aren't autograd-tracked, they may not even appear in the autograd graph!).\n\n**Important technical subtlety**: the standard torch.optim.Adam step runs under torch.no_grad(); therefore **no autograd graph nodes are generated at all**. TorchViz's make_dot renders the autograd graph rooted at loss. The optimizer's update ops are not in that graph. This is the major confounder for the entire question. How to resolve: (a) use functional/grad-mode Adam: if the update is computed in grad mode as part of a differentiable loss (like a differentiable optimizer), ops are tracked and appear in the graph; (b) TorchViz has `make_dot` that can render arbitrary Grad_fn graphs. TorchOpt is designed for functional transforms; with functorch/torch.func and make_from_functorch, updates can be made traceable in grad mode. Honest solution: the design must define a \"differentiable step harness.\" Here the Adam update is computed in grad mode (as in meta-learning usage, like TorchOpt's documented differentiable-optimizer mode), so the update ops enter the autograd graph and torchviz renders \"the gradient graph including the optimizer path.\" Rungs: eager per-op in grad mode, foreach (note: foreach under grad mode — _foreach_ functions may or may not track gradients; multi-tensor kernels under no_grad...). Hmm, foreach/fused kernels typically don't support grad-mode tracking. So the ladder may need adjustment: only per-op implementations produce a graph that appears in grad mode; fused kernels are opaque by design — that's exactly the point: \"what does the renderer see\": nothing/an opaque node. \n\nDesign resolution: define two capture protocols and state them explicitly:\n- Protocol A (update ops in-graph): the optimizer step is computed in grad mode via a functional formulation; each rung renders whatever the autograd graph sees. If a rung's fused kernel doesn't track gradients, its rendered footprint is an opaque boundary node — count as 1 fused node + boundary edges (normalization rule).\n- Protocol B (trace-based): capture the implementation via torch.fx / torch.jit trace / torchopt's transform trace regardless of gradient tracking, and measure the IR of the traced program.\nAnd separately state the trivial-but-true fact: under standard non-differentiable usage, no rung adds anything to the autograd graph; report this as a caveat to question validity.\n\nHmm — the researcher said \"don't pad scope beyond what the question needs.\" Shouldn't proliferate protocols. Simpler: pick the grad-mode functional harness as the sole in-graph protocol (this is the only setting where \"the gradient graph of a fused Adam\" has content, and it's TorchOpt's documented differentiable-optimizer mode). The trace representation for TorchOpt's jit-trace is handled via fx/jit capture as a second representation to be reported separately.\n\nRungs:\n- R0: backward graph with loss only (floor).\n- R1: eager per-op functional Adam (in grad mode) — per-op manipulation.\n- R2: foreach/multi-tensor — partial: batched across parameters, not fused within updates; note possible gradient-tracking constraints → may be excluded by the equivalence gate or harness constraints; label as \"partial rung.\"\n- R3: fused kernel Adam (torch fused=True) — single kernel; in grad mode likely opaque/no gradient → rendered as boundary; partial manipulation of rendered complexity, opaque by design.\n- R4: TorchOpt functional Adam (grad mode, stateless API) — the framework arm; mechanism: functional decomposition, optionally with a fused backend flag.\n- R5: TorchOpt transform/JIT-captured (e.g., jit via make_jit / torch.compile) — mechanism: graph capture/fusion via compiler; **redundancy caveat**: may internally lower to torch.compile / same inductor fused kernel as R3 → not independent from R3; treat as a superset check of R3, not a separate independent arm for attribution claims.\n\nAlso explicitly: the torch.compile(Adam) arm overlaps with R5; fold into R5.\n\n### Normalization Contract\nIR: directed graph; node = {ir_op_type, arity, is_fused_boundary, label, source_format}; edge = data dependency. Rules:\n\nMapping table (representative rows):\n| Native label (examples) | IR type | Notes |\n- aten::mul / mul / operator.mul → MUL\n- aten::add (tensor-tensor) → ADD; aten::add (scalar/alpha attribute) → ADD\n- aten::sqrt → SQRT; aten::pow(0.5) → SQRT\n- aten::addcmul → ADDCMUL (or map to composite? keep native composite as 1 node with canonical name)\n- aten::div, aten::add_ / add (in-place) → ADD (in-place flag stripped)\n- aten::exp, log, maximum (eps clamp) → respective canonical name\n- aten::_multi_tensor_adam / foreach variants → FUSED_UPDATE(opset=foreach), single node per call, but arity = 2*P tensors: count as 1 node but also record fan-in; report both raw and per-parameter counts\n- Fused CUDA kernel boundary (non-differentiable) → FUSED_UPDATE(opset=aten_fused), boundary node + edges at touched parameters\n- torch.jit/inductor fused kernel (e.g., triton_per_fused_* / fused_adam) → FUSED_UPDATE(opset=compiled)\n- TorchOpt primitives (adam_update, scale_by_adam...) → canonical decomposition if exposed, else single node\n- Constants/attributes: get_attr, constant tensors (lr, eps, betas) → **discard** from complexity node count, record number of discarded constants.\n- Guards (jit guard nodes, check_*): **discard** from node/edge counts; report number discarded (guards affect readability but not math).\n- Noops/aliasing (detach, shallow-copy, view) → **contract** (collapse).\n- Getitems/tuple packing from foreach unpack: contract.\n\nReporting rules: per-graph node count AND per-parameter normalized count = (nodes attributable to P parameter updates)/P; primary metric is per-parameter count to prevent arity from masquerading as fusion; foreach's 1 node × 2P fan-in vs eager's 1 node × 2 fan-in × P: raw node count massively favors foreach, but math is unchanged. Residual bias: (a) raw counts still reported and could be cherry-picked; (b) boundary-node collapse makes fused arms structurally simpler *by counting rule*, not by construction — must state that part of the measured simplification is definitionally entailed by the contract; (c) constant-discard removes guard-heavy compile noise that could arguably be part of interpretability; (d) label readability is not captured by node counts.\n\n### Capture Protocol\n- Fixed MLP (seeded), fixed synthetic batch (generated with seeded torch.randn; 64×64), fixed hyperparameters (lr=1e-3, betas=(0.9,0.999), eps=1e-8), seeds {0,1,2}, captures at steps 1, 5, 10.\n- Rendering: torchviz.make_dot on the loss (and, if needed, on post-update loss or parameter grads of step N under the grad-mode harness), styled DOT with graphviz 12.x binary; fixed rendering parameters.\n- TorchOpt trace: torch.jit.trace / torch.fx.symbolic_trace or torchopt.make_jit export → text → parse to IR.\n- Repeat captures: run each arm × seed × step 3 times; check determinism of IR (graph hash). Non-deterministic arms: node-count distribution, report median and max deviation; if within-arm variance > between-arm difference → flag the difference as unresolved. Pre-registered comparison unit: the (arm, model, step-capture) triple aggregated over seeds; primary contrast is R1 vs R4 (fusion state at same framework boundary)... hmm, actually the manipulation of fusion within a framework: R1 vs R3 is within-PyTorch; the R4 vs R1 vs R5 TorchOpt comparison crosses frameworks — used for framework context, not attribution. Pre-registered primary contrast: per-graph and per-parameter IR node counts of R1 vs R3 (both torch, differ only in fusion), with R5 as a confirmatory extension; the TorchOpt-vs-TorchViz question is answered by: the complexity of R5's own trace representation and the rendered graph under R4 — reported separately, not averaged.\n\n### Numerical Equivalence Gate\n- Before graph analysis: run each arm for 100 steps with identical seed; compare parameter tensors and loss trajectory against R1 baseline: max abs diff ≤ 1e-6 (parameters near O(1)), relative ≤ 1e-5 where abs fails; fused kernels get a looser tolerance of 1e-5/1e-4 per documented precision differences (fp32 non-associativity). Arms failing → exclude from graph comparison; report separately as \"non-comparable math\" with divergence step.\n\n### Analysis Plan\n- Metrics per capture: node count (raw, per-parameter), edge count, distinct IR op types, longest path, fused-boundary count, compression ratio vs R1, label readability (mean character length; fraction of labels in a human-curated readable whitelist), rendered layout: number of edge crossings counted from SVG via node/edge coordinate parsing (or via pygraphviz cross count after xdot), number of ranks/layers from dot layout, SVG byte size.\n- Non-determinism: within-arm CV across seeds; use median across seeds for each arm for each (metric, step); Mann–Whitney U or exact permutation test on seed-level paired differences for pre-registered R1 vs R3 contrast (n=3 seeds → treat as descriptive; power for n=3 is minimal, so honestly: report effect sizes and intervals, don't over-rely on p-values).\n- Pre-registration: lock the metric list, normalization table, and contrasts in the protocol repository before running; publish graph hashes.\n\n### Interpretability Module (optional, separately costed; default: OFF)\n- Default state: OFF; in that case all interpretability claims are labeled \"untested\" and only structural/readability/layout proxies are reported.\n- Fallback if no raters: proxies only, explicitly labeled.\n- When enabled: ≥5 raters familiar with gradient descent, blinded, order randomized; 10-item task (count update ops, locate momentum state, Likert ease-of-understanding rating); Krippendorff's α ≥0.67 as an aggregation reliability gate; analysis: mixed model with rater as random effect.\n\n### Feasibility\n- Nothing executed; environment audit confirmed absence of both libraries, no torch, py3.14, no network, no dot binary. Execution requires: py 3.10–3.12, torch ≥2.2 (for foreach/fused maturity and functorch in-core), torchopt (any version ≥0.x with stateless Adam and make_jit), torchviz, graphviz binary, networkx/pygraphviz, single CUDA GPU (fused rungs require CUDA; no CUDA → R3/R5-fused marked as missing, partial execution possible), CPU-only fallback for R0/R1/R2/R4. Total compute is trivial (tiny model; minutes).\n- All numerical predictions are unspecified; empirical direction is undetermined.\n\nAlso, state the \"standard usage caveat\": with the common no-grad optimizer usage, torchviz's graph contains no optimizer ops for any rung — the design's grad-mode functional harness is a necessary operationalization; state this explicitly.\n\nI think I'm ready to write the result. Structured and complete, but not verbose. Use tables where useful. The researcher's requirements map to sections. Length: since this is the final deliverable, it can be thorough — probably 900–1300 words in the answer. Let's write it.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Does Optimizer-Side Fusion Produce a Simpler, More Interpretable Gradient Graph?\n\n## 0. Question as operationalized\nTorchViz is the **measurement instrument** (renderer of PyTorch's autograd graph → DOT/SVG); **optimizer fusion state** is the manipulated variable. TorchOpt's captured transform trace and TorchViz's rendered autograd graph are **different representations** and are reported as **separate measurements** — never averaged into one \"graph complexity\" score.\n\n**Validity caveat stated up front:** in conventional usage `optimizer.step()` runs under `no_grad`, so *no* rung contributes optimizer nodes to the autograd graph and the question would be vacuous. The design therefore uses a **grad-mode functional-step harness**: the Adam update is computed differentiably in grad mode (the mode TorchOpt documents for differentiable optimizers), so update ops are tracked and TorchViz renders \"the gradient graph including the optimizer path.\" All complexity comparisons are conditioned on this harness.\n\n## 1. Fusion ladder (manipulated variable, mechanism per rung)\n\n| Rung | Arm | Mechanism varied | Independence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R0 | Loss-only backward graph (no optimizer ops) | Floor: how many nodes the update adds at all | baseline |\n| R1 | Eager per-op functional Adam (`foreach=False, fused=False`) | Per-element eager aten ops | **baseline arm** |\n| R2 | `foreach=True` (multi-tensor Adam) | **Partial manipulation**: batches *across parameters*, does not fuse ops *within* an update | partial rung, labeled as such |\n| R3 | `fused=True` native fused CUDA Adam | Single-kernel update; CUDA-only; gradient-impermeable → renders as one opaque boundary node | independent *mechanism*, but its rendered simplicity is partly definitional (see §3 bias note) |\n| R4 | TorchOpt functional Adam (grad-mode, stateless API) | Framework arm: functional decomposition of the update | crosses framework — used for context, **not** for fusion attribution |\n| R5 | TorchOpt transform/JIT-captured step (`make_jit`/compile path) | Compiler graph capture + fusion | **Flagged redundancy**: may lower to the same Inductor/fused kernel as R3. Treated as a confirmatory superset of R3, not an independent attribution arm |\n\nAttribution of \"fusion → simpler graph\" rests on **R1 vs R3** (same framework, one mechanism apart), with R2 as the partial-fusion probe and R5 as confirmation. R4 answers the TorchOpt-side question; R0 calibrates.\n\n## 2. Stimuli, controls, capture protocol\n- Model: 3-layer MLP (64→32→32→1, ReLU), MSE loss on a fixed synthetic tensor (seeded `randn`, 64×64). Hyperparameters fixed for all arms: lr=1e-3, betas=(0.9,0.999), eps=1e-8. Seeds {0,1,2}; captures at optimizer steps **1, 5, 10**; 3 repeated captures per (arm, seed, step).\n- Identical input batch and seed across arms; identical TorchViz version, DOT styling, and `dot` layout settings for every render.\n- TorchOpt trace representation captured separately per run (fx/jit text export of the transformed step) and parsed into the same IR — reported as its own measurement series alongside the TorchViz-rendered graph for R4/R5.\n\n## 3. Normalization contract (deliverable component)\nIR: directed graph; node = {ir_op_type, arity, is_fused_boundary, label, source_format}; edge = tensor data dependency. Parsed from DOT and from trace text into networkx DiGraphs; all metrics computed only on IR.\n\n**Op-identity mapping table (locked before any run):**\n\n| Native label(s) | IR type | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `aten::mul`, `operator.mul`, `*` | MUL | collapse |\n| `aten::add`, `add_`, `addcmul` | ADD, ADDCMUL | inplace flag stripped; `addcmul` kept as one composite node |\n| `aten::sqrt`, `pow(x,0.5)` | SQRT | collapse |\n| `aten::div`, `exp`, `log`, `maximum`, `clamp` | DIV/EXP/LOG/MAX | canonical names |\n| `aten::_multi_tensor_adam*` / foreach ops | FOREACH_UPDATE | **one node**, arity = fan-in recorded |\n| fused CUDA kernel / grad-impermeable boundary | FUSED_UPDATE(native) | one boundary node with edges at touched tensors |\n| Inductor/Triton `fused_adam*`, TorchOpt jit output | FUSED_UPDATE(compiled) | one node |\n| TorchOpt `scale_by_adam`/`adam_update` when decomposed | decomposition's canonical ops | expand if source exposes them |\n| constants & attrs (`lr`, `eps`, betas, `get_attr`) | — | **dropped** from counts; drop-count reported |\n| jit guards (`check_*`, assertions) | — | **dropped** from counts; drop-count reported |\n| `detach`, aliases, `getitem`/tuple-packing no-ops | — | **contracted** |\n\n**Counting rule:** every metric reported **both** per-graph and **per-parameter** (nodes attributable to P parameter updates ÷ P). Per-parameter is primary: one FOREACH_UPDATE node with 2P fan-in is arithmetically P updates and must not beat eager per-op counts on raw node count by arity alone.\n**Residual biases (stated):** (a) raw per-graph counts still exist and remain cherry-pickable; (b) collapsing fused kernels to one boundary node makes R3/R5 structurally simpler *by construction of the contract* — part of any measured \"fusion benefit\" is definitionally baked in, and the drop-counts (guards/constants) quantify what the contract hides; (c) label readability and layout effects are not captured by node counts at all.\n\n## 4. Numerical-equivalence gate (runs first)\n100 steps per arm, identical seed; compare parameter and loss trajectories to R1: max |Δparam| ≤ 1e-6 absolute OR ≤ 1e-5 relative (O(1) params); fused/compiled arms get 1e-5/1e-4 per documented fp32 non-associativity. **A failing arm is excluded from all graph comparisons and reported separately as \"non-comparable math,\" with divergence step.** Arms that cannot satisfy grad-mode tracking (a hard harness constraint, not an equivalence failure) are reported as harness-incompatible, with their rendered footprint limited to the boundary-node rule.\n\n## 5. Metrics and analysis plan\n- **Structural (on IR):** node count (raw + per-param), edge count, distinct IR op types, longest path/DAG depth, fused-boundary count, compression ratio vs R1 at matched step.\n- **Readability proxies:** mean label char-length; fraction of labels in a pre-declared human-readable whitelist (vs mangled names).\n- **Rendered layout (from SVG/xdot with fixed styling):** edge crossings, rank/layer count, canvas area, SVG bytes.\n- **Pre-registered comparison unit:** the (arm, step-capture) triple, aggregated as the **seed-median** across seeds {0,1,2}. Pre-registered primary contrast: **R1 vs R3, per-parameter node count at step 5**, with matched-seed paired differences. Non-determinism: graph-hash each capture; within-arm variance (CV across seeds and repeats) reported per metric; any between-arm gap smaller than within-arm spread is declared **unresolved** rather than assigned a direction. With n=3 seeds, hypothesis tests (Wilcoxon signed-rank on paired seeds) are reported alongside effect sizes and confidence intervals, labeled as underpowered; conclusions rest on effect size + determinism checks, not p-values.\n- **Pre-registration:** metric list, mapping table, contrasts, and whitelist frozen in the protocol repo before any run; raw graph hashes and IR files published.\n\n## 6. Interpretability module — OPTIONAL, separately costed; **default: OFF**\n- **Default (OFF):** no human study. All claims of \"more interpretable\" are labeled **UNTESTED**; only structural/readability/layout proxies are reported, explicitly flagged as proxies that do not measure interpretability.\n- **Fallback if raters unavailable after activation:** identical to OFF state.\n- **If enabled:** ≥5 raters familiar with gradient descent; blinded, randomized arm order per item; per figure: 5-point ease-of-understanding Likert plus two objective tasks (count update ops in the figure; locate the momentum state). Aggregation gated on Krippendorff's α ≥ 0.67; analysis = mixed model with rater as random effect. Primary conclusion never incorporates proxy scores; the module's result is reported as a separate claim.\n\n## 7. Feasibility and environment (verified by read-only audit; nothing executed)\nThis machine: `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; TorchOpt and TorchViz are **absent entirely** (no checkouts, no packages, no wheels, no vendored pins); torch/graphviz-binary/networkx also absent; Python 3.14.4; network disabled — so coexistence/version-conflict analysis from actual metadata files was **impossible** and any dependency statements remain memory-based assumptions. Execution elsewhere requires: Python 3.10–3.12, torch ≥2.2, torchopt (stateless Adam + jit transform), torchviz, graphviz `dot` binary, networkx; one CUDA GPU (R3 and the fused path of R5 are CUDA-only; without GPU those rungs are **missing rungs**, and a partial run of R0/R1/R2/R4 is explicitly permitted and labeled partial). Compute is trivial: minutes on CPU, seconds per step on GPU. **All complexity numbers are unspecified predictions; no experiment was run and none is claimed; whether fusion simplifies the graph remains an open empirical question in either direction.**\n\n## 8. Rationale (one paragraph)\nThe question conflates a renderer with an optimizer, so the design holds the renderer constant and manipulates fusion within one framework (R1→R2→R3), which is the only comparison that attributes graph changes to fusion rather than framework. Because fused arms are opaque by construction, the normalization contract with per-parameter primary counting prevents arity artifacts from masquerading as fusion wins, and its own definitional biases are quantified rather than hidden. Interpretability is deliberately quarantined so the design's unavoidable default (no raters) yields an honest \"untested,\" and the pre-registered contrasts plus determinism checks convert what would otherwise be a figure-aesthetic argument into a small, falsifiable measurement.", "evidence": "Repository/environment observations grounding feasibility and the \"nothing executed\" status: (1) `ls -la /workspace` → only `task.json`; no TorchOpt/TorchViz checkouts, no examples, no pinned versions. (2) `pip show` + `importlib.util.find_spec` → torch, torchopt, torchviz, graphviz, networkx all absent; `dot` binary absent (exit 1). (3) Filesystem-wide `find / -xdev` for torchopt*/torchviz*/functorch*/*.whl → zero hits; dependency metadata files (setup.py/pyproject) do not exist locally, so conflict analysis from files was impossible — flagged in §7. (4) Python 3.14.4, network disabled per environment block → design-only output is consistent with `task.json` note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Design content per researcher's five requirements: §1 ladder with per-rung mechanism, partial-rung labels (R2), and R3/R5 redundancy flag; §3 written op-identity table, constant/guard drop rules, contract rules, per-parameter primary counting, and three named residual biases; §4 tolerance basis (1e-6 abs / 1e-5 rel; fused 1e-5/1e-4) with exclusion-and-separate-reporting on failure; §6 interpretability module default OFF with no-rater fallback and proxies explicitly non-substitutive; §5 pre-registered comparison unit (arm×step, seed-median), determinism via graph hashes and within-arm spread threshold; §7 execution-environment list and explicit statement that all numbers are unspecified predictions with open empirical direction.", "assumptions": ["Memory-based (unverifiable locally, repos absent): TorchO